| RAML root | endpoint paths, HTTP methods, uri/query params, declared response codes, security scheme | resolved via §4.1 lookup chain |
| RAML examples | seed request/response bodies | `examples/*.raml`, inline `example:` blocks (under the resolved RAML root) |
| MUnit suites | success/error case names, mocked status codes, headers/queryParams/uriParams | `src/test/munit/*-test-suite.xml` `<munit:set-event>` |
| MUnit fixtures (request) | actual request JSON bodies | `src/test/resources/testdata/{suite}/business-inbound/*-request.json` |
| MUnit fixtures (response) | expected response bodies for Postman `response[]` documentation array | `src/test/resources/testdata/{suite}/business-outbound/*-response.json` |
| Source flow XML | detect non-HTTP message sources for the §6 pre-filter | `src/main/mule/*.xml` |
| Project POM | discover the `{api-name}-spec` artifact coordinates (groupId, artifactId, version) | `pom.xml` `<dependencyManagement>`, `<dependencies>` |
| MuleSoft MCP `search_asset` | locate the API spec asset in Anypoint Exchange | runtime |
| MuleSoft MCP `list_applications` | live CloudHub `fullDomain` per env | runtime |
| MuleSoft MCP `list_api_instances` (fallback) | API Manager `consumerEndpoint` if app is fronted by API Manager | runtime |
| Local Maven cache | RAML artifact unpacked or as `*.zip` | `~/.m2/repository/{groupId-path}/{api-name}-spec/{version}/` |
| **Test case workbook** | TC ID, expected HTTP status, description, pre-reqs | `project/input_postman/*.xlsx` (auto-discovered in §3.0) |
| **Seed successful payloads** | one per non-health endpoint, from user/MUnit/RAML | captured in §3.1 batch, resolved via §6.5 chain |
| **Live-resolved seed payloads (Tier 0)** | real Salesforce Ids/values auto-derived from app code; optional pre-step | `project/input_postman/{api}-seed-payloads.json` (written by `agents/testdata` resolver; see §6.5 Tier 0) |
| **Mutation library** | trigger patterns, payload transforms, header transforms, default status codes | embedded in §6.11 of this rule |

### 4.1 RAML Spec Lookup Chain (CRITICAL)

The agent must resolve the canonical RAML root by walking this chain in order. **Stop at the first hit.** Record which tier resolved it and surface it in a chat note (e.g., `"Resolved RAML from local Maven cache: ~/.m2/repository/.../sfmc-consents-sys-api-spec/1.0.5/"`).

#### Tier 1 — POM Dependency Management

Read `pom.xml` (project root) and its `<parent>` POM if present. Look in `<dependencyManagement>` and `<dependencies>` for an artifact whose `<artifactId>` matches `{api-name}-spec` (often with `<classifier>raml</classifier>` or `<type>raml-fragment</type>`/`<type>zip</type>`).

Capture:
- `groupId` (e.g., `com.insulet`)
- `artifactId` (e.g., `sfmc-consents-sys-api-spec`)
- `version` (e.g., `1.0.5`)
- `classifier` / `type` if present

This determines the artifact path used in Tier 3.

#### Tier 2 — Anypoint Exchange (via MCP)

Call the MuleSoft MCP to search Exchange:

```
CallMcpTool: search_asset
  server: user-mulesoft
  arguments:
    searchQuery: "{api-name}-spec"
    assetFilters: ["rest-api"]
    statuses: ["published"]
    maxResults: 5
```

Match an asset whose `assetId` (or `name`) equals `{api-name}-spec` and whose `groupId` matches the value from Tier 1 (if Tier 1 succeeded). If found, this is the canonical published source — note the version pinned in Exchange and prefer the same version as the POM (Tier 1) when available.

If the agent only has Exchange metadata (asset id + version) but no local file, it must still proceed to Tier 3 to materialize the bytes from the local Maven cache (Exchange artifacts are downloaded into `~/.m2` when Maven resolves them).

#### Tier 3 — Local Maven Cache

Compute the cache path:

```
~/.m2/repository/{groupId-with-slashes}/{api-name}-spec/{version}/
```

For example, `groupId=com.insulet.api`, `artifactId=sfmc-consents-sys-api-spec`, `version=1.0.5` resolves to:

```
/Users/sbajpayee/.m2/repository/com/insulet/api/sfmc-consents-sys-api-spec/1.0.5/
```

Look for, in order:

1. An unpacked directory containing `{api-name}-spec.raml` at its root.
2. A `*.zip` artifact (e.g., `sfmc-consents-sys-api-spec-1.0.5-raml.zip` or `...-raml-fragment.zip`). If only the zip exists, unzip it to a temp directory and use the unpacked RAML root.
3. A `*-raml.zip` or `*.jar` containing RAML resources under `/api/` or at the root.

If the version is unknown (Tier 1 missed), list the directory and pick the highest semantic version available locally.

#### Tier 4 — Workspace RAML Examples Folder

Check `agents/raml/examples/{api-name}-spec/`. Look for the root RAML file `{api-name}-spec.raml` or any `*.raml` matching the pattern.

#### Tier 5 — In-Project Fallback

Check `src/main/resources/api/` for a root RAML. This is the legacy in-project location.

#### Tier 6 — ASK the User

If all five tiers miss, ASK the user via `AskQuestion` to provide:
- The path to the RAML root file, OR
- The Exchange asset id + version (and the agent will retry Tier 3 with the supplied coordinates).

If the user skips, abort generation with a clear chat error — the rule cannot proceed without a RAML spec.

