## 5. MuleSoft MCP URL Resolution Algorithm

For each environment the user selected, resolve the public base URL using a strict **three-tier fallback**: MCP → ASK user → placeholder. Never silently fail.

### 5.1 Tier 1 — MCP

Call `list_applications` first (the canonical CloudHub source):

```
CallMcpTool: list_applications
  server: user-mulesoft
  arguments:
    environmentName: <ENV>     // e.g., "DEV"
    appName: <api-name>        // e.g., "sfmc-consents-sys-api"
```

Defensively read the response — the MCP tool descriptors document only inputs, so access fields with optional chaining and try multiple shapes:

```
fullDomain =
     application?.fullDomain
  || target?.deploymentSettings?.http?.inbound?.publicUrl
  || data?.[0]?.fullDomain
  || data?.[0]?.application?.fullDomain
```

If `fullDomain` resolves, the base URL is `https://{fullDomain}` (CloudHub default scheme).

If `list_applications` returned nothing or no domain field could be extracted, fall back to:

```
CallMcpTool: list_api_instances
  server: user-mulesoft
  arguments:
    environmentName: <ENV>
```

Match an instance whose `assetId` / `instanceLabel` / `name` corresponds to the API and read `consumerEndpoint` (or equivalent URL field).

### 5.2 Tier 2 — ASK user

If MCP resolution fails for any env, you **must** ask the user (do not silently move to a placeholder). Use `AskQuestion` with one prompt per unresolved env, batched into a single question form. Example prompt:

> "MCP could not resolve a public URL for `QA`. Paste the base URL (e.g., `https://my-api-qa-xxxx.usa-e1.cloudhub.io`), or choose 'Skip' to use a TODO placeholder."

### 5.3 Tier 3 — Placeholder (last resort)

Only if the user skips or provides no value, set the collection variable to a literal placeholder:

```
{ "key": "base_url_<ENV>", "value": "TODO-set-base-url-for-<ENV>" }
```

Surface a single chat warning listing every env that fell through to placeholder, e.g., `"WARNING: base_url_QA and base_url_UAT were not resolved — set them in the Variables tab before running."`.

### 5.4 Decision Diagram

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Start[For each selected env] --> Call1["list_applications<br/>environmentName=ENV<br/>appName=API"]
    Call1 --> Found{Has fullDomain?}
    Found -->|Yes| Extract["base_url_ENV<br/>= https://{fullDomain}"]
    Found -->|No| Call2["list_api_instances<br/>environmentName=ENV"]
    Call2 --> Match{Match by label/asset?}
    Match -->|Yes| Extract2[Read consumerEndpoint]
    Match -->|No| AskUser["AskQuestion: paste base URL<br/>for ENV (or skip)"]
    AskUser --> Provided{User provided?}
    Provided -->|Yes| UseUserUrl[Use user-supplied URL]
    Provided -->|Skipped| Placeholder["base_url_ENV<br/>= 'TODO-set-base-url-for-ENV'"]
    Extract --> Variable[Set collection variable]
    Extract2 --> Variable
    UseUserUrl --> Variable
    Placeholder --> Variable
```

### 5.5 Notes

- No `list_environments` tool exists in the MuleSoft MCP — env names are user-supplied constants from §3.
- The `local` env always uses `http://0.0.0.0:8081` (or `http://localhost:8081`) — do **not** call MCP for it.

## 6. MUnit Data Extraction Algorithm

When `testCaseFile == null`, follow this exact procedure (§6.1–§6.10). When `testCaseFile` is set, §6.1–§6.4 and §6.5 still run, then skip to §6.11 for test-case-driven mutation. In both modes §6.12 runs last.

### 6.1 Step 1 — Glob suites

Glob `src/test/munit/*-test-suite.xml`. Process each suite file in turn.

### 6.2 Step 2 — Pre-filter non-HTTP-triggered flows (CRITICAL)

Inspect the suite's `<munit:set-event>` attributes **and** look up the source flow it tests in `src/main/mule/*.xml`. If the flow's source is **not** an HTTP listener — i.e., it is `anypoint-mq:subscriber`, `scheduler`, `file:listener`, `jms:listener`, `vm:listener`, or any other event-driven source — **skip the entire suite**. These flows have no HTTP entry point and therefore no Postman equivalent.

**Detection signals (from the `<munit:set-event>` attributes shape, per `gen-munit-implementation.mdc` §"Message Source Attribute Structures"):**

| Attribute shape | Source | Action |
|---|---|---|
| `headers`, `queryParams`, `uriParams`, `requestPath`, `method` | `http:listener` | **Include** |
| `properties.correlationId`, `messageId`, `contentType` | `anypoint-mq:subscriber` | **Skip** |
| Bare payload only, no attributes | `scheduler` | **Skip** |
| `path`, `filename`, `size`, `timestamp` | `file:listener` | **Skip** |
| JMS / VM / custom message attributes | `jms:listener`, `vm:listener`, etc. | **Skip** |

**Cross-check:** open the source flow XML referenced by the suite (usually `<flow-ref>` to `{api}-main` or a named flow) and confirm the source element. If you find `<anypoint-mq:subscriber>`, `<scheduler>`, `<file:listener>`, `<jms:listener>`, `<vm:listener>`, etc., skip the suite.

**Reporting:** emit a single chat note after processing all suites listing every skipped suite and the reason, e.g.:

```
Skipped non-HTTP suites:
- order-mq-consumer-test-suite.xml — flow source is anypoint-mq:subscriber
- nightly-batch-test-suite.xml — flow source is scheduler
```

### 6.3 Step 3 — Classify tests

Parse each `<munit:test name="...">` in the surviving suites and classify by name pattern (per `gen-munit-implementation.mdc` §"File Naming Convention"):

- `*-success*` / `*-positive*` / `*-happy*` → **happy-path bucket**
- `*-error*` / `*-validation-error*` / `*-not-found*` / `*-unauthorized*` / `*-server-error*` / `*-bad-request*` → **negative bucket**

Map the scenario keyword to an HTTP status using **this fixed allow-list table**. Codes outside this list are dropped:

| Scenario keyword in test name | HTTP status emitted |
|---|---|
| `validation-error`, `bad-request`, `invalid` | `400` |
| `unauthorized`, `auth-error`, `missing-credentials` | `401` |
| `not-found`, `missing` | `404` |
| `server-error`, `internal-error`, `unhandled-error` | `500` |
| anything else (e.g., `forbidden`, `duplicate`, `unprocessable`, `conflict`, connectivity) | **DROP — do not emit** |

### 6.4 Step 4 — Extract request shape from `<munit:set-event>`

Inside `<munit:execution>` find the first `<munit:set-event>`. From `attributes` extract:

- `headers` → seed Postman `request.header[]` (with secrets stripped per §8)
- `queryParams` → seed `request.url.query[]`
- `uriParams` → seed `request.url.variable[]`
- `requestPath` + `method` → seed URL path and `request.method`

### 6.5 Seed Successful Payload Resolution Chain

This step resolves one canonical successful request body per non-health endpoint. It runs for **every** execution path — both workbook-driven and MUnit-driven — because the seed payload is the starting point for all mutation (§6.11) and the fallback body for all synthesized required-negative requests (§6.12).

Health endpoints (`/ping`, `/health-check`, `/health-check/ready`, `/health-check/live`) bypass this section — they remain body-less per §9.

For each non-health `(METHOD, path)` pair discovered from the RAML, resolve the seed payload in this priority order. **Stop at the first hit.**

0. **Tier 0 — Live-resolved seed payloads (highest priority).** Before attempting any other tier, check whether `project/input_postman/{api}-seed-payloads.json` exists (where `{api}` is the API name in use). If the file exists:
   - Parse it as JSON. Locate the `endpoints["{METHOD} {path}"]` entry for this endpoint (e.g. `"POST /api/v1/memberships"`).
   - If the entry is present and `happy.body` is a non-empty, parseable JSON value, use `happy.body` as the seed payload. Record `_provenance` attribution in chat (e.g., `"Tier 0 live-resolved: campaignName=Campaign.Campaign_Key__c, leadId=Lead.Id — org: integration_campaign_devint2"`).
   - If the entry also provides `negative.400.body`, store it in the negative-400 slot so §6.12 need not synthesize a generic one for this endpoint (still verify the real-id + bogus-key pattern from the resolver is present).
   - Store in `seedPayloads[{method, path}]` and **stop** — do not fall through to Tiers 1–3.
   - If the file exists but has no entry for this endpoint (or `happy.body` is empty/null), emit a chat note: `"Tier 0 file found but no entry for {METHOD} {path} — falling back to Tier 1."` Then continue to Tier 1.

   > **When to run the resolver:** The `project/input_postman/{api}-seed-payloads.json` file is produced by the `agents/testdata` Live Test Data Resolver (`/use-testdata`). Run it before `/use-postman` for any API with POST/PUT endpoints that must fire against a live Salesforce org. The resolver is optional — if the file is absent, this tier is skipped and the existing chain continues unchanged.

1. **Tier 1 — User-supplied JSON** (captured in the §3.1 batch). Validate it parses as valid JSON; check that required RAML body schema keys are present and warn (but accept) on mismatch. Store in `seedPayloads[{method, path}]`.
2. **Tier 2 — MUnit fixture** — `src/test/resources/testdata/{suite}/business-inbound/*-success*-request.json`. If multiple `*-success*.json` exist for the same endpoint, pick the largest non-empty file by byte size.
3. **Tier 3 — RAML example** — inline `example:` block or `examples/{endpoint}-request.raml` under the resolved RAML root for that endpoint + method combination.
4. **Hard stop** — if none of Tiers 0–3 yields a parseable body, abort that endpoint with a chat error listing the missing inputs: `"Cannot generate requests for POST /api/v1/foo — no seed payload found. Run /use-testdata to generate a live-resolved seed, or provide JSON, add a MUnit success fixture, or add a RAML example."` **Do not** silently emit an empty body or a placeholder.

### 6.6 Step 5 — Inline request body

Resolve any `readUrl('classpath://testdata/{suite}/business-inbound/...json')` reference to the actual file under `src/test/resources/testdata/{suite}/business-inbound/`. Inline its content into `request.body.raw`.

### 6.7 Step 6 — Populate the response example

Locate `<munit-tools:assert-equals>` / `<munit-tools:assert-that>` referencing `attributes.statusCode` or expected response payloads. Use this to:

- Set the response example name and `code` in the Postman `response[]` array.
- Populate `response[*].body` from the matching `business-outbound/*-response.json`.

### 6.8 Fallback chain

If a fixture is missing for a given endpoint × scenario:

1. Fall back to the RAML `examples/*.raml` for that endpoint.
2. If no RAML example exists, ASK the user via `AskQuestion` to paste a sample payload.
3. If the user skips, **do not emit a placeholder body** — drop the request and surface a chat note.

### 6.9 Per-Endpoint, Per-Use-Case Tailoring

Emitted negative scenarios from MUnit are derived **strictly from what exists** for each endpoint. This section applies in the MUnit-driven path (`testCaseFile == null`). The required-negatives floor (§6.12) subsequently fills any gaps.

- For each endpoint discovered in the RAML, build the initial MUnit-derived negative set as the **intersection** of:
  - (a) the four-code allow-list `{400, 401, 404, 500}`, and
  - (b) the codes that have a corresponding MUnit test or RAML `responses:` entry for *that specific endpoint*.
- `500` is only included when evidenced by MUnit/RAML — it is never synthesized by §6.12.
- After §6.12 runs, every non-health endpoint will additionally carry at least one `400`, `401`, and `404` regardless of MUnit evidence.

### 6.10 Deduplication

If multiple MUnit tests cover the same `endpoint × scenario`, emit **one** Postman request, not one per test. Choose the test whose fixture has the most complete payload (largest non-empty `business-inbound` JSON).

### 6.11 Test-Case-Driven Payload Mutation

This section runs **only when `testCaseFile` is set**. It replaces the MUnit-derived negative scenario discovery (§6.3 / §6.9) for the negative set (happy-path remains MUnit-derived). After this section completes, §6.12 runs to enforce the required-negatives floor.

#### Algorithm

For each data row in the workbook (skip the header row):

1. **Parse columns:** `TC_ID` (Col A, e.g., `NGASIM-3143_TC02`), `expected_status` (extracted from Col A via pattern `_(\d{3})_` or from a dedicated status column), `description` (Col B), `pre_reqs` (Col H if present).
2. **Filter** rows whose `expected_status` is outside `{400, 401, 404, 500}` — drop with a chat warning: `"Dropped TC_ID {X} — status {Y} outside allow-list {400, 401, 404, 500}."`
3. **Resolve target endpoint** via the description: match HTTP method + path tokens against the RAML endpoint list. If ambiguous, ask the user via `AskQuestion` before proceeding.
4. **Retrieve seed payload** from `seedPayloads[{method, path}]` (resolved in §6.5).
5. **Match the description against the mutation library** (§6.11 table below, priority order — first match wins). Each entry specifies the trigger regex, the payload transform, the header transform (if any), and the default expected status code.
6. **If no library entry matches**, apply **LLM field-aware mutation**: parse the description for a field name + constraint (e.g., `"lastName exceeds 80 chars"` → set `lastName` to a 200-char string; `"phone number starting with 8"` → set first digit to `8`). Cross-check the inferred status against `expected_status` from Col A — if they disagree, emit a chat warning and trust the workbook value.
7. **If LLM cannot identify a field/constraint**, ask the user once via `AskQuestion` to provide a mutated payload for that TC. If skipped, drop the request and surface a chat note. **Never** emit the unmutated seed payload for a non-2xx scenario unless the matched library entry explicitly marks it "payload unchanged" (`invalid-credentials`, `dependency-outage`).
8. **Build the request** using `expected_status` from the workbook as the **authoritative** `response[*].code` (overrides any library default on conflict).
9. **Name the request** `{TICKET}_{TC_ID}_{HTTP_STATUS}_{SHORT_TITLE}` where `SHORT_TITLE` is the first 6–8 words of Col B (e.g., `NGASIM-3143_TC02_400 Bad Request_Missing Mobile Number`). Copy the full Col B text verbatim into `request.description` with a citation: `Source: {TICKET} Col B / gen-postman-implementation-v2.mdc §6.11`.
10. If Col H (`pre_reqs`) is non-empty, prepend its content to `request.description` as a `PRE-REQUISITE:` block.

#### Canonical Mutation Library

New mutation patterns must be added to this table **only** — never inlined directly into collections.

| Library entry | Trigger regex (applied to lowercased description) | Payload transform | Header transform | Default status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `phone-starts-with-8` | `phone\s*(number)?\s+(starting\|starts)\s+with\s+['"]?8` | Replace first digit of `mobileNumbers[0]` / `phoneNumber` / `mobile` with `8` (preserve total length) | — | `400` |
| `invalid-sf-id` | `invalid\s+sf\s*(id\|account\|lead)` or `invalid\s+salesforce\s+id` | Replace the Salesforce ID field (`sfId`, `accountId`, `leadId`, `contactId`, `personaId`, or uri param `{id}`) with `"INVALID000000000"` | — | `404` |
| `datatype-violation` | `data[\s-]*type\s+validation`, `type\s+mismatch`, `wrong\s+type` | Pick the first required scalar field from the RAML body schema; if it is a string, replace its value with a number (e.g., `1`); if a number, replace with a string (e.g., `"invalid"`) | — | `400` |
| `missing-required-field` | `(missing\|without\|removing\|no)\s+(\w+)\s+(field\|key\|attribute)` | Capture the field name from the match group; delete the matching top-level or nested key from the seed payload | — | `400` |
| `field-exceeds-max-length` | `exceed(s\|ing)?\s+max(\s+length)?`, `over\s+\d+\s+char` | Pad the named field with `"x"` to 2× its declared RAML `maxLength` (or 200 chars if unknown) | — | `400` |
| `empty-payload` | `empty\s+(payload\|body\|request)` | Replace body with `{}` | — | `400` |
| `invalid-credentials` | `invalid\s+credentials`, `wrong\s+client[_\s-]*(id\|secret)`, `unauthorized.*invalid` | Payload unchanged | Override `client_id` header value → `"invalid-client-id"` and `client_secret` header value → `"invalid-client-secret"` (the **only** sanctioned exception to §8) | `401` |
| `dependency-outage` | `outage`, `downstream\s+(failure\|unreachable)`, `host\s+(misconfigured\|unreachable)` | Payload unchanged | — | `500` |
| `non-existent-resource` | `non[- ]?existent`, `unknown\s+(id\|resource)` | Set the path uri param `{id}` to `"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"` (if uuid-shaped) or `"NONEXISTENT001"` (if string-shaped) | — | `404` |

For the `dependency-outage` entry, prepend to `request.description`: `"NOTE: This test requires a backend config change to reproduce. See pre-requisite column for details (e.g., set SFMC host property to an incorrect value)."`

### 6.12 Required-Negatives Floor & Synthesis

This section runs **in both modes** (workbook-driven and MUnit-driven), immediately after all row-by-row processing and §6.9/§6.11, and immediately before §10.2 validation.

#### Required floor (per non-health endpoint)

| Code | Scenario | Required? |
|---|---|---|
| `400` | Bad Request — validation / APIKit / missing field / datatype | **Yes** |
| `401` | Unauthorized — invalid credentials | **Yes** |
| `404` | Not Found — invalid resource id / non-existent path param | **Yes** (with one sanctioned gap — see below) |
| `500` | Internal Server Error — dependency outage | No — evidence-driven only |

Health endpoints (`/ping`, `/health-check`, `/health-check/ready`, `/health-check/live`) are **exempt** — happy-path only, unchanged from v1 behavior.

#### Coverage-check pass

For each non-health `(METHOD, path)` endpoint, compute the set of already-emitted negative status codes. For each code in `{400, 401, 404}` that is missing, synthesize one request using the §6.11 library default:

- **Missing `400`** → apply `missing-required-field` against the first required scalar field declared in the RAML body schema. If the endpoint has no request body (e.g., GET with required query/uri params), apply `datatype-violation` — flip the type of the first required query/uri param.
- **Missing `401`** → apply `invalid-credentials` (override `client_id`/`client_secret` headers with the sanctioned literal values; seed payload unchanged).
- **Missing `404`** → apply `non-existent-resource` against the uri param `{id}` if one exists; otherwise apply the `invalid-sf-id` transform against the first id-like body field (`*Id`, `*_id`, or `id`). If **neither** a uri param nor an id-like body field exists for this endpoint, **skip the 404 synthesis** and surface a chat warning:
  ```
  WARNING: POST /api/v1/foo — cannot synthesize 404 (no uri param or id-like field found).
  Add a workbook row or accept this gap.
  ```

Synthesized requests are named as follows:

- When a workbook ticket prefix is known: `{TICKET}_SYN_{code}_{short-endpoint-label}` (e.g., `NGASIM-3143_SYN_401_consents-opt-ins`)
- Otherwise: `synth-{method}-{path-slug}-{code}` (e.g., `synth-post-consents-opt-ins-401`)

Every synthesized `request.description` must include:

```
Source: synthesized — required-negatives floor (gen-postman-implementation-v2.mdc §6.12).
No workbook row or MUnit test evidenced this {code} for this endpoint.
```

#### Chat report

After the coverage-check pass, emit a single chat note (even if empty) — this signals the floor ran:

```
Required-negatives floor (§6.12) — synthesis report:
- POST /api/v1/consents/opt-ins — 401 synthesized (invalid-credentials)
- GET  /api/v1/consents/{id}   — 404 synthesized (non-existent-resource)
[No gaps for: POST /api/v1/consents/opt-outs]
```

If no synthesis was needed, emit: `"Required-negatives floor (§6.12): all endpoints already covered — no synthesis needed."`

## 7. Postman Collection Schema

The output must be a Postman v2.1 collection. Use this skeleton:

```json
{
  "info": {
    "name": "{api-name}",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json",
    "description": "Auto-generated from RAML + MUnit. Set anypoint_client_id and anypoint_client_secret in the Variables tab before running."
  },
  "variable": [
    { "key": "base_url_local", "value": "http://0.0.0.0:8081" },
    { "key": "base_url_DEV",   "value": "<resolved-or-placeholder>" },
    { "key": "base_url_QA",    "value": "<resolved-or-placeholder>" },
    { "key": "base_url_UAT",   "value": "<resolved-or-placeholder>" },
    { "key": "base_url_PROD",  "value": "<resolved-or-placeholder>" },
    { "key": "anypoint_client_id",     "value": "", "type": "string" },
    { "key": "anypoint_client_secret", "value": "", "type": "secret" },
    { "key": "sourceSystem", "value": "<from-user>" },
    { "key": "targetSystem", "value": "<from-user>" }
  ],
  "item": [
    {
      "name": "DEV",
      "item": [
        {
          "name": "Happy Path",
          "item": [
            /* one request per endpoint, one per success status */
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Negative — 400 / 401 / 404 / 500 (floor + evidenced per endpoint)",
          "item": [
            /* one request per (endpoint × negative scenario), including §6.12 synthesized */
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
    /* additional env folders (QA, UAT, PROD, local) follow the same shape */
  ]
}
```

### 7.1 Per-Request Shape

Every request emitted must conform to:

```json
{
  "name": "{METHOD} {path} — {scenario}",
  "request": {
    "auth": { "type": "noauth" },
    "method": "POST",
    "header": [
      { "key": "client_id",        "value": "{{anypoint_client_id}}",     "type": "text" },
      { "key": "client_secret",    "value": "{{anypoint_client_secret}}", "type": "text" },
      { "key": "x-correlation-id", "value": "{{$guid}}",                  "type": "text" },
      { "key": "sourceSystem",     "value": "{{sourceSystem}}",            "type": "text" },
      { "key": "targetSystem",     "value": "{{targetSystem}}",            "type": "text" },
      { "key": "Content-Type",     "value": "application/json",            "type": "text" }
    ],
    "body": {
      "mode": "raw",
      "raw": "<inlined business-inbound JSON>",
      "options": { "raw": { "language": "json" } }
    },
    "url": {
      "raw": "{{base_url_DEV}}/api/v1/{path}",
      "host": [ "{{base_url_DEV}}" ],
      "path": [ "api", "v1", "{path-segment}" ],
      "query": [ /* from <munit:set-event> queryParams */ ],
      "variable": [ /* from <munit:set-event> uriParams */ ]
    }
  },
  "response": [
    {
      "name": "{scenario} — {statusCode}",
      "originalRequest": { /* mirror of request */ },
      "code": 200,
      "status": "OK",
      "_postman_previewlanguage": "json",
      "header": [ { "key": "Content-Type", "value": "application/json" } ],
      "body": "<inlined business-outbound JSON>"
    }
  ]
}
```

### 7.2 Path & Naming

- **Default format:** `{METHOD} {path} — {scenario}` — e.g., `POST /api/v1/consents/opt-ins — Happy Path`, `POST /api/v1/consents/opt-ins — 400 Validation Error`.
- **Workbook-driven format:** `{TICKET}_{TC_ID}_{HTTP_STATUS}_{SHORT_TITLE}` — e.g., `NGASIM-3143_TC02_400 Bad Request_Missing Mobile Number`. Col B is copied verbatim into `request.description` with a `Source:` citation.
- **Synthesized-floor format:** `{TICKET}_SYN_{code}_{short-endpoint-label}` (workbook run) or `synth-{method}-{path-slug}-{code}` (MUnit run). Description includes the §6.12 synthesis citation.
- Folder structure: `<ENV>` → `Happy Path` | `Negative — ...` → individual requests.
- One Postman request per `endpoint × scenario`, per env.

## 8. Security Rules (Hard Checklist)

Verify all items before writing the file. **Abort generation if any check fails.**

- `client_id` and `client_secret` headers ALWAYS reference `{{anypoint_client_id}}` / `{{anypoint_client_secret}}`. **Never** copy literal values from MUnit fixtures, properties files, or the reference Postman collection (which contains hardcoded secrets and must NOT be replicated verbatim).
- Bearer tokens use `{{bearer_token}}` only. Never inline a real JWT.
- The collection-level `variable[]` for `anypoint_client_secret` uses `"type": "secret"` so Postman masks it in the UI.
- **Pre-write secret scan:** scan the generated JSON for any value matching the regex `[A-Za-z0-9_\-]{16,}` adjacent to a key matching `client_secret|password|api_key|api[_-]?token|access[_-]?token|bearer|token` (case-insensitive). If any non-template value is found, abort and report the offending location. **Exception:** the two sanctioned literal values from the `invalid-credentials` mutation entry (`"invalid-client-id"` and `"invalid-client-secret"`) are the ONLY values exempt from this scan — they are short enough to not trip the 16-character threshold and are required for the 401 test to work.
- Do not include OAuth `account_id`, real `access_token`, or any signed JWT pulled from MUnit fixtures.
- Mask obvious PII from MUnit `business-inbound` fixtures the same way `review-logging-standards.mdc` masks payloads — phone numbers (`17742581354` → `1XXXXXXXXXX`), emails (`user@example.com` → `u***@example.com`), account IDs.

### 8.1 Anti-Pattern Reference

The reference collection `agents/postman/examples/sfmc-consents-sys-api.postman_collection.json` (if present) contains hardcoded credentials at lines 20, 52, 167, and 172. These are **anti-patterns**. Use the file for **structural** reference only — never copy header values verbatim.

## 9. Standard Header Set

Every business request must include these headers (referenced via collection variables, never literals):

- `client_id: {{anypoint_client_id}}`
- `client_secret: {{anypoint_client_secret}}`
- `x-correlation-id: {{$guid}}` (Postman dynamic variable, randomized per send)
- `sourceSystem: {{sourceSystem}}`
- `targetSystem: {{targetSystem}}`
- `Content-Type: application/json` (only when a request body is present)

Health endpoints (`/ping`, `/health-check`) get only `x-correlation-id`.

## 10. Output Location & Validation

### 10.1 Output Path

Primary: `agents/postman/examples/{api-name}.postman_collection.json`.

If a Mule project root is detected (via `pom.xml` + `src/main/mule/`), also offer to write a copy to `<project-root>/postman/{api-name}.postman_collection.json`. Ask the user before writing the second copy.

### 10.2 Post-Generation Validation Checks

Run these checks after building the JSON, before writing:

- `info.schema` is exactly `https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json`.
- Every `request.url.raw` parses to a valid URL whose `host[]`/`path[]` arrays match the `raw` string (no orphaned segments).
- The §8 secret scan finds zero non-template values (the `invalid-credentials` literal pair is the only sanctioned exception — verify it appears only in `401`-type negative requests).
- At least one happy-path request exists per HTTP endpoint.
- **Required-negatives floor:** every non-health endpoint emits at least one request each for `400`, `401`, and `404`. The only sanctioned gap is `404` for endpoints that lack both a uri param and an id-like body field — that gap must be reported in chat (§6.12). Fail validation if any other floor gap exists.
- **Status-code allow-list:** every emitted negative request has `response[*].code` in `{400, 401, 404, 500}`. Any other code (`403`, `409`, `422`, `502`, `503`, `504`, timeouts, etc.) anywhere in the file fails validation.
- **Workbook coverage (when `testCaseFile` is set):** every workbook row with a valid status code maps 1:1 to an emitted request. Any unmapped row is listed in a chat warning before writing.
- **Seed payload resolved:** every non-health endpoint has a seed payload recorded in `seedPayloads`. Endpoints absent from this map are absent from the output — not stubbed.
- **Tier 0 live-resolved values:** when `project/input_postman/{api}-seed-payloads.json` was used as Tier 0, verify that every `happy.body` value used is non-empty and attributed in chat with the org alias and field provenance from `_provenance`. Any field that resolved to an empty string or null must be treated as a resolution failure — omit that endpoint and surface a chat note.
- **No event-driven flows:** the file contains no requests derived from MQ/scheduler/file/JMS-triggered flows (those are pre-filtered in §6.2).
- Every request references `{{base_url_<ENV>}}` for its host — no literal CloudHub domains in `request.url.host[]`.

If any check fails, fix the issue and re-run validation. Do not write a non-conformant file.

## 11. Anti-Patterns (DO NOT)

- **DO NOT** hardcode `client_id` / `client_secret` / bearer tokens.
- **DO NOT** replicate `Bearer eyJ...` JWTs from MUnit fixtures or other collections.
- **DO NOT** emit any status code outside the strict allow-list `{400, 401, 404, 500}` for negatives — including `403`, `409`, `422`, `502`, `503`, `504`, and timeouts.
- **DO NOT** blanket-apply all four negative codes to every endpoint based on §6.9 alone — §6.12 handles the required floor via synthesis, and `500` is never synthesized.
- **DO NOT** generate Postman requests for **non-HTTP-triggered flows** — flows whose source is `anypoint-mq:subscriber`, `scheduler`, `file:listener`, `jms:listener`, `vm:listener`, or any other event-driven source must be skipped entirely (§6.2).
- **DO NOT** embed real account IDs, tenant IDs, or PII from MUnit `business-inbound` fixtures (mask per §8).
- **DO NOT** use `0.0.0.0` as a host outside the `local` env folder.
- **DO NOT** generate one-request-per-MUnit-test — deduplicate to one Postman request per `endpoint × scenario` (§6.10).
- **DO NOT** silently fail URL resolution — always follow the three-tier fallback in §5.
- **DO NOT** skip the seed-payload resolution chain (§6.5) for any non-health endpoint — run it in both workbook and MUnit modes. Abort that endpoint if no body resolves.
- **DO NOT** emit a non-2xx scenario with the unmutated seed payload unless the matched §6.11 library entry explicitly marks it "payload unchanged" (`invalid-credentials`, `dependency-outage`).
- **DO NOT** omit required negatives `{400, 401, 404}` for any non-health endpoint — synthesize via §6.12 if the workbook/MUnit didn't cover them.
- **DO NOT** treat a workbook row as a substitute for the required floor — the floor is per-endpoint, not per-workbook. A workbook covering only `400`s still triggers §6.12 synthesis for the missing `401` and `404`.
- **DO NOT** invent test cases beyond the §6.12 synthesized minimum when a workbook is supplied — the workbook is the closed set for everything except the required floor.

